@@s001 Filters: average, correlation, convolution
@@s002 Position ${n}: the window covers ${f[n - 1]}, ${f[n]}, ${f[n + 1]}. Inner product with the filter: ${somma}.
@@s003 dato:signal,ora:window under the filter,due:filter,ok:output
@@s004 A signal of 8 samples: a <b>peak</b> (the 9) and a <b>step</b> (from 3 to 6). The <b>moving average</b> filter has 3 equal weights, 1/3 each.
@@s005 Result: the peak 9 has become 5, 5, 5 (spread out: <b>noise attenuated</b>); the sharp step 3 → 6 has become a ramp 4, 5, 6 (<b>blurred edge</b>). At the two ends the window goes outside the signal: with <code>mode="valid"</code> those values are not computed.
@@s006 dato:noisy signal,due:average over 3,ok:average over 9,spento:true signal
@@s007 dato:signal,ora:window,due:filter,ok:output
@@s008 dato:image (one row),ora:pixels on the edge,ok:output of the filter
@@s009 dato:signal,spento:zeros added,ok:output
@@s010 Filters: average, correlation, convolution
@@s011 Lecture 7. A filter repeats the same operation at each point of a signal or an image, looking only at a small neighbourhood (local processing). Each output is an <b>inner product</b> between the weights of the filter and the nearby values. The exercises are in week 8 (<i>Constructing filters</i>, filtering the gaze); exam question <b>Q6 (a)</b>. PM chapter 5, DW chapter 13.
@@s012 What filtering is for
@@s013 <b>Removing noise</b> (smoothing, denoising): random noise changes from one sample to the next, the signal does not. Taking local averages attenuates the noise.
@@s014 <b>Extracting information</b>: edges, variations, directions (derivatives, week 8), similarity to a model (template matching).
@@s015 One filters signals over time (audio, the coordinate of the pupil frame after frame), images (2D), volumes (3D).
@@s016 Filtering is always a compromise
@@s017 The same filter that attenuates the noise also attenuates the real details: the sharp edge becomes a ramp. The wider the filter, the more noise it removes and the more detail it loses. For the gaze: too much smoothing and the real jumps of the eye (saccades) become slow.
@@s018 Local average: less noise, less detail
@@s019 The moving average, step by step
@@s020 Moving average of 3 samples
@@s021 Result verified with <code>np.convolve(f, np.ones(3)/3, "valid")</code>.
@@s022 Each output is an inner product
@@s023 Weights of the filter for the values of the window, then the sum.
@@s024 It is the <a href="w02-prodotto-interno.html">inner product</a> between the vector of the weights and the vector of the window. Weighted sum = linear combination: the filter is a <b>linear</b> operation.
@@s025 The centre counts most: the nearest sample is the most reliable for estimating the value there. With these weights the output at position 2 is (3 + 18 + 3)/4 = 6. A weight much larger than the others ⇒ the filter almost does nothing (it copies the centre).
@@s026 If all the values are 7, the output is 7·(sum of the weights) = 7. That is why smoothing filters sum to 1: they do not change the average brightness.
@@s027 Correlation and convolution
@@s028 They are two almost identical formulas for doing the same thing: sliding a filter <span class="m">h</span> over the signal <span class="m">f</span> and summing the products. The symbol <span class="m">y<sub>n</sub></span> is the result at position <span class="m">n</span>. <span class="m">Σ<sub>m</sub></span> (sigma) means "sum over all the values of <span class="m">m</span>". <span class="m">h<sub>m</sub></span> is the value of the filter at position <span class="m">m</span>. <span class="m">f</span> with an index is a value of the signal. The only thing that changes is the sign of the index of the signal: <span class="m">n + m</span> in the correlation, <span class="m">n − m</span> in the convolution.
@@s029 The only difference: the filter is flipped
@@s030 The filter slides over the signal as it is.
@@s031 The same thing with the <b>flipped</b> filter (in 2D, rotated by 180°). It is the formula of DW 13.
@@s032 Flipped is the same as itself. That is why in smoothing the difference does not show.
@@s033 With a non-symmetric filter the sign changes. Verified with <code>np.convolve</code> and <code>np.correlate</code>. It is the reason why the tutorial writes the derivative filter as [1, −1] "the other way round".
@@s034 So: <b>convolving with [1, −1]</b> gives the same result as <b>correlating with [−1, 1]</b>, that is with the flipped filter.
@@s035 The convolution of an impulse is the filter itself
@@s036 Convolving an image with a single lit pixel gives a copy of the filter centred there. This is how the filters of Task 1 are built (<i>shifting pixels</i>): with <code>scipy.ndimage.convolve</code>, a 1 to the right of the centre moves the image to the right; with <code>correlate</code> it moves it to the left (verified on a 5 × 5 image).
@@s037 Why the course says "filter" and not "kernel"
@@s038 In many books the filter is also called kernel. In the course "kernel" already indicates the feature map of the models (Φ(x) = [1, x, x²], <a href="w05-kernel.html">week 5</a>). Saying "filter" avoids confusing the two things.
@@s039 The same filter, flipped: the sign changes
@@s040 2D filters
@@s041 The same idea on an image: a small matrix of weights slides over each pixel; each output pixel depends on its neighbourhood (3 × 3, 5 × 5, …).
@@s042 Filter
@@s043 Weights
@@s044 Sum
@@s045 Effect
@@s046 <b>average</b> 3 × 3
@@s047 all 1/9
@@s048 blurs uniformly
@@s049 <b>Gaussian</b> 3 × 3
@@s050 blurs, giving more weight to the centre
@@s051 Gaussian 5 × 5 (Task 2)
@@s052 blurs more (wider)
@@s053 <b>sharpening</b> K₂ (Task 4)
@@s054 9 at the centre, −1 around
@@s055 emphasises the differences from the neighbourhood
@@s056 <b>Sobel</b> x (Task 5)
@@s057 responds to vertical edges (week 8)
@@s058 Sums computed with NumPy. On a constant image (all 7) average, Gaussian and sharpening give 7, Sobel gives 0: where nothing changes, a derivative filter responds 0.
@@s059 Task 4: why sharpening works
@@s060 It can be rewritten as centre + 8·(centre − average of the neighbours).
@@s061 Weights with sum 1: the flat image does not change.
@@s062 The bright pixel next to a dark one becomes brighter, and vice versa: the edges look sharper. Noise is amplified too.
@@s063 Template matching (Task 3)
@@s064 A piece of the image (the template, for example a sunflower) is used as a filter and the <b>correlation</b> with the whole image is computed: where the window resembles the template the inner product is large. The maximum of the map is the best position. The template is a very simple "model": an inner product used as a measure of similarity. <code>skimage.feature.match_template</code> uses the normalised version, which does not reward zones merely because they are bright. Tried: in an image with a cross (the template) and a very bright square, the raw correlation chooses the square (value 25), <code>match_template</code> the cross (value 1.0, the maximum possible).
@@s065 Average, sharpening and Sobel on an edge
@@s066 In code
@@s067 The functions of the exercises
@@s068 With <code>"valid"</code> only the positions where the filter lies entirely inside the signal are kept: 8 − 3 + 1 = 6 outputs. With the default <code>"full"</code> there are 10, completed with zeros.
@@s069 The 1D Gaussian filter used in the exercise on the gaze: σ decides the width, that is how much it blurs.
@@s070 In 2D: by hand with a matrix of weights, or with the ready-made Gaussian of scikit-image.
@@s071 Q6 (a)
@@s072 "Filtering (1D, 2D, and n-D convolution and correlation, blurring and smoothing, and noise)." The path: noise → local average → filter as inner product → correlation vs convolution → 2D filters and sum of the weights → noise/detail compromise → the gaze example (<a href="w08-sguardo-filtrato.html">week 8</a>).
@@s073 Ready sentences
@@s074 "Each output sample is the inner product between the filter weights and the local neighbourhood of the input."
@@s075 "Convolution flips the filter; for symmetric filters such as the Gaussian it gives the same result as correlation."
@@s076 valid versus full
